EXCEPTIONS(1)              KERNEL EXCEPTIONS FUNCTIONS            EXCEPTIONS(1)NAME       exceptions - kernel exceptions definitionsSYNOPSIS       exceptions ( -- )       exception ( --- )       exception> ( [signal] or [exception] -- ) compilation immediate       raise ( [signal] or [exception] -- )       (exception;) ( -- ) compilation       (exceptionunlink;) ( -- ) compilation       (exception>) ( -- ) compilationDESCRIPTION       Kernel support word set for exception definition and management.       Exceptions may be signaled by the environment (such as arithmetic       errors or memory segmentation error) or raised by functions in       an application.exceptions ( -- )       Vocabulary containing the exception management definitions.       Allows definitions of forth level capturing of errors. Uses       a syntax similar Ada to make the beginning of an exception       section within a colon definition.exception ( --- )       Used in the form:               exception <exception-name>       To define an exception symbol. An exception may be raised using:               <exception-name> raise       The defined exception returns a pointer to itself when used.(exception;) ( -- ) compilation       Compiled by "exception>" to end a code definition block and       unlink the current exception frame.(exceptionunlink;) ( -- ) compilation       Compiled by "exception>" to end a code definition block with       an argument frame and local variables. Unlinks the exception       frame and the argument frame.(exception>) ( -- ) compilation       Compiled by "exception>" to handle the run-time activity       of building an exception frame and jumping to the beginning       of the definition.exception> ( [signal] or [exception] -- ) compilation immediate       Used within a colon definition to mark the beginning of the       exception part of the definition:               : <name> .... exception> ( exception -- ) .... ;       If an error occurs or an exception is raised during the       execution of the definition part the control is past to the       exception block and the signal number or the exception is       past as a parameter. The status of the stacks are restore to       the situation before the execution of the most recent definition        with an exception block. To pass the exception upwards the        function "raise" should be used.       raise ( [signal] or [exception] -- )       Activates a exceptions handler with signal identification number        or an exception symbol. If an exception frame exists the virtual       machine is restored to the state and resumed. The default action       taken if no exception block is available is abort with a message       about the task, and the name of the signal or exception. Signals        are generated from the run-time environment.SEE ALSO       forth(1)COPYING       Copyright (C) 1989 Mikael R.K. Patel       Permission is granted to make and distribute verbatim copies       of this manual provided the copyright notice and this permission       notice are preserved on all copies.       Permission is granted to copy and distribute modified versions       of this manual under the conditions for verbatim copying,        provided also that the section entitled "GNU General Public       License" is included exactly as in the original, and provided       that the entire resulting derived work is distributed under       the terms of a permission notice identical to this one.       Permission is granted to copy and distribute translations of       this manual into another language, under the above conditions       for modified versions, except that the section entitled "GNU       General Public License" may be included in a translation approved       by the author instead of in the original English.AUTHOR       Mikael R.K. Patel       Computer Aided Design Laboratory (CADLAB)       Department of Co